Abstract = {This study compares the body perceptions of pregnant teenagers with
   those of their never-pregnant peers.  Forty-three third-trimester
   pregnant girls were matched by age, race, socioeconomic status, and
   pubertal development to 43 never-pregnant girls.  Subjects were given
   the Tennessee Self-Concept Scale (TSCS), a self-administered
   questionnaire consisting of 100 self-descriptive statements which are
   divided into nine subscales.
   When compared with never-pregnant girls, the pregnant girls have a
   higher overall self-esteem, a more positive body image, a surer
   self-identity, and feel more productive as a family member, but appeared
   limited in their capacity for self-criticism.},
