Note = {5th International Conference on Environmental Science and Information
   Application Technology (ESIAT), Hong Kong, HONG KONG, NOV 07-08, 2014},
Abstract = {Water supply reservoirs in urban Southern Africa are becoming more prone
   to pollution due to rapid urbanisation, resulting in the deterioration
   of the quality of raw water abstracted from these reservoirs. The
   deterioration is mainly due to algal blooms which impart taste and odour
   to the water. There is therefore need for water service providers to be
   able to predict the distribution of these pollutants in order to
   optimise treatment and produce safe and aesthetically acceptable water
   to the consumers. Numerical models are useful in prediction and they
   require as part of input, bathymetry data for the reservoir. This paper
   sought to describe the generation of the bathymetry for Vaal Dam
   reservoir for use in a numerical model for future water quality
   investigations. This was achieved by use of specialised software. It was
   concluded that application of spatial information systems is essential
   for water quality management},
