   Social Sciences, LOMONOSOV MOSCOW STATE UNIV, MOSCOW, RUSSIA, APR 25-MAY
   06, 2000},
Organization = {NATO},
Abstract = {A central tenet of cognitive psychology and of psychodynamic psychology
   is the existence of internalized mental representations, which the
   mind/brain utilizes in order to perceive the environment and generate
   appropriate behaviors in response to those perceptions. Serious
   questions and challenges have been raised concerning the existence of
   mental representations, their necessity in mental processes, the
   functional organizational level at which they are manifested and
   supported, and the mechanisms for their manifestation and operation.
   This article will survey a variety of possible representational schemes,
   the known neurobiological evidence and model mechanisms underlying each,
   most of which depend in some fashion or another upon the existence of
   nonlinear processes.},
