Note = {100th Annual Meeting of the American-Psychological-Association,
   WASHINGTON, DC, AUG   15, 1992},
Organization = {Amer Psychol Assoc},
Abstract = {The Family-of-Origin Scale (FOS) is a 40 item, ten subscale self-report
   instrument designed to assess perceptions of family health. The scale,
   based on the dimensions of Autonomy and Intimacy, has demonstrated
   reliability and validity with adolescents. The FOS was administered to
   100 adolescent psychiatric inpatients concurrently with the Brief
   Symptom Inventory (BSI) to examine the scale's discriminant and
   construct validity. When compared with 100 non-clinical adolescents, the
   psychiatric inpatients perceived their family as less healthy on all FOS
   dimensions. Perceived family health on the FOS was negatively and
   moderately correlated with the BSI dimensions. The findings provide
   psychometric and clinical support for the FOS with adolescents.},
