@inproceedings{ WOS:000431842100016,
Author = {McKay, Jennifer},
Editor = {Lukasiewicz, A and Dovers, S and Robin, L and McKay, J and Schilizzi, S and Graham, S},
Title = {Australian jurisprudence of justice in water management: present
   limitations, future issues and law reform suggestions},
Booktitle = {NATURAL RESOURCES AND ENVIRONMENTAL JUSTICE: AUSTRALIAN PERSPECTIVES},
Year = {2017},
Pages = {215-232},
Note = {Workshop on Justice, Fairness and Equity in Natural Resource Management,
   Australian Natl Univ, Fenner Sch Environm \& Soc, Canberra, AUSTRALIA,
   OCT 12-13, 2015},
Organization = {Acad Social Sci},
Abstract = {This chapter describes several justice theories and counterposes these
   with a series of actual decisions in key water management cases. The
   decisions occurred under state laws and the Water Act 2007 and hence the
   relevant laws differ. Hence, the first point that this chapter makes
   about justice is, that the differences between the relevant laws,
   creates differences and these may lead to injustice between residents of
   different states. This would be in the nature of a different outcome on
   the same facts. This arises from the constitutional settlement in 1901,
   which entrenched state pre-eminence in this domain and others such as
   criminal law. Hence, distributive justice is not achieved by this
   process of having different state laws. Procedural and informational
   justice are also a product of the different state systems too, and hence
   also differ from state to state. Therefore justice is not served well by
   having so many different rules about water management in Australia. For
   example, all urban water supply businesses provide relief to customers
   if they cannot pay their water bill, but use different criteria.
   Overall, the state level focus has impeded national thinking about water
   management.
   The change to this was the radical adoption of the Water Act 2007, which
   provides limited oversight of state laws in the Murray-Darling region.
   This Act imposes a clear human rights statement that requires critical
   human water needs to be the first priority.
   This chapter deals with rural water use for production and regional
   water allocation plans that attempt to create sustainable use that
   ensures ecologically sustainable development (ESD).
   This chapter then considers the gaps between the theory and practice. It
   asks what has been the outcome of the laws and the processes and applies
   them to rural water management in Australia over the last 200 years, and
   then suggests some institutional and law reforms to make the future more
   just. It uses studies of key cases adjudicating disputes over powers to
   change consumptive water uses to achieve (ESD) in the new instrument,
   called `regional water plans'. The Australian states have many names for
   these, and many hundreds of plans exist. The chapter decides that ESD is
   a conception of justice that incorporates present and future generations
   and finds that ESD has been operationalised very well through judges.
   The chapter however, suggests reform along the lines of Corporations
   Law: that is, that governments should pass identical state laws with
   uniform definitions. This chapter is a personal reflection on our
   greatest natural resource, water, and how it is the basis for all
   wealth.
   Wealth is evidently not the good we are seeking; for it is merely useful
   and for the sake of something else. (Aristotle(1))
   My reflection on the legal system is that we must think like a nation
   with regard to water management. Water is our commons, and the greatest
   offence to common sense is to manage it using political boundaries.
   Water supply is a human right and it should be the first role of
   government to provide water in a sustainable way that preserves the
   environment for future generations.
   They hang the man and flog the woman
   That steal the goose from the common
   But let the greater villain loose
   That steal the common from the goose.
   (English folk poem, Anonymous)},
